{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed (i.e., where a higher probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones). To assess skewness: Lottery A has two outcomes—4000 EUR with 80% probability (favorable outcome) and 0 EUR with 20% probability (unfavorable outcome). Since the higher probability (80%) is on the favorable outcome, Lottery A is left-skewed. Lottery B offers 3000 EUR with 100% probability, so it has no variance and thus no skewness (it is a degenerate distribution). Therefore, neither lottery is right-skewed; in fact, A is left-skewed and B is symmetric (no skew). The decision reason would not prefer either based on being 'more right-skewed'—if anything, it would disprefer A because it is not right-skewed. However, the reason could still be applied in principle by evaluating the skewness of each lottery. The relevant information (outcomes and probabilities) is available, so the reason can be applied to the problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual expresses a concern about anticipated regret: they would regret getting nothing in Lottery A when they could have secured 3000 EUR with certainty in Lottery B. They emphasize that the money is windfall (not part of their financial plans), so losing it would not create a hardship, but missing the guaranteed gain would lead to self-blame. The justification centers on emotional consequences (regret avoidance) and the psychological value of certainty, not on distributional properties like skewness. The individual considers the possibility of getting 0 in A and compares it to the sure gain in B, focusing on the worst-case scenario and its emotional impact. There is no mention of probability distributions, shape of lotteries, or comparative frequency of good vs bad outcomes beyond the single unfavorable outcome of 0 EUR.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on evaluating the skewness of the outcome distributions—specifically, whether unfavorable outcomes carry higher probability than favorable ones. The individual does not analyze the lotteries in terms of skewness or distributional shape. Instead, they focus on the emotional cost of regret in the case of a zero outcome under A versus the security of B. While the individual does consider that A has a chance of yielding nothing (an unfavorable outcome), this is not framed in terms of skewness but in terms of counterfactual thinking and emotional response. The justification is affective and based on anticipated regret, not on structural analysis of probability distributions. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual applied the concept of skewness or preferred a more right-skewed lottery. The reasoning is orthogonal to the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

