  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and comparing the skewness of the two lotteries and preferring the more right-skewed one (Lottery B). While the individual ends up choosing B, the justification does not involve any analysis of the distribution of outcomes or probabilities. The individual does not reference the shape of the distribution, the concentration of probability on low outcomes, or any concept related to skewness. Instead, the choice is framed as arbitrary and emotionally justified through a narrative of fate and humor. There is no evidence that the individual considered the information relevant to skewness (e.g., comparing probability masses on favorable vs unfavorable outcomes). The justification is entirely non-analytical and existential. Therefore, although the final choice aligns with what the decision reason would recommend, the reasoning process is fundamentally different.",
