{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed (i.e., has a higher probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones). To assess skewness, we need to compare the distribution of outcomes and their probabilities. Lottery A: -500 Euros with 100% probability — this is a degenerate (certain) loss, so it has no variance and thus no skewness in the traditional sense, but can be considered perfectly symmetric (or undefined skewness). Lottery B: -1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability — this is a two-point distribution with equal probabilities. The outcomes are losses or zero, so both outcomes are non-positive. The larger loss (-1000) has only 50% chance, while the better outcome (0) also has 50%. Since the worse outcome has the same probability as the better one, this is symmetric in probability mass around the midpoint (-500), but because the larger magnitude outcome is worse, the distribution is left-skewed (negative skew) — more mass on the right (better outcomes). Right-skew would imply more mass on the left (worse outcomes), which is not the case here. Therefore, neither lottery is right-skewed; if anything, B is left-skewed, and A is certain. The decision reason, which prefers more right-skewed lotteries, would not clearly favor either, but technically, since right-skew means higher probability on worse outcomes, A (certain -500) might be seen as more 'right-skewed' than B, which has a 50% chance of no loss. However, skewness is not typically defined for such small discrete distributions in a straightforward way. Still, the information needed (outcomes and probabilities) is available, so the reason can be applied in principle.",
